Stage 10: the crossing-count reconciliation with the Rust slice. R5.

commit:  b64797755da62536e50ed4021271fc3efb53b773 (+ this slice)
core:    ffi/poc/codec built WITH --features count, so ak_enc_counters reports.

THE CONVENTIONS NEVER DIFFERED. This slice previously reported 2 forward where
the Rust slice reports 8 for P1.2 encode, and recorded it as an unresolved
difference of convention. It is not one. Both count the same things, and the
numbers below are read from the CORE's own ak_enc_counters, which is the same
counter the Rust slice reads.

The difference is CHUNK SIZE, which is a host choice the ABI leaves open.
ffi/logs/rust/stage2-four-arms-M1.log reports forward 2, 3 and 8 for 4, 300 and
1000 elements. Subtracting the one ak_encode_* call leaves 1, 2 and 7
ak_elem_ResultRaw calls, which is exactly ceil(n/150). The Rust host chunks its
run at 150 elements; this slice's host hands the whole run over in one call.

Set AK_CHUNK=150 and this slice reproduces the Rust slice's counts TO THE DIGIT:

---------- AK_CHUNK=0 ----------
                                                      host      core (R5)
payload  elements    bytes  encode  decode  value   fwd  rev    fwd   rev  note
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  chunk = the whole run elements per ak_elem_* call

P1.1            4      858  ok      ok      ok        5    4      4     2  
P1.2         1000   218121  ok      ok      ok        5    4      4     2  
P1.3          300      605  ok      ok      ok        5    4      4     2  absent path
  host is this binding's own tally of calls it issued. Useful for checking the
    binding does what it thinks, and NOT comparable with any other slice.

  The host tally is what this slice reported before, and reporting it against
  the Rust slice's number was comparing two different quantities.
  They are CONSTANT in the element count, in BOTH directions, whether the
  payload carries 4 elements or 1,000. The 5 and 4 cover an encode, a decode
  and a re-encode:
    encode     2 forward (ak_encode_*, ak_elem_*)  1 reverse (loop_results)
    decode     1 forward (ak_decode_*)             2 reverse (apply, add_results)
  ResultRaw is a LEAF, so encode hands the whole run over in one ak_elem_*
  call and decode gets it back in one add_results. That is ABI v1's batching
  predicate doing the thing it exists for, on .NET.

