The core columns above cover an encode AND a re-encode, so halve them for one
encode. At AK_CHUNK=150 that is forward 2 / 8 / 3 for P1.1 / P1.2 / P1.3 against
the Rust slice's 2 / 8 / 3, and reverse 1 / 1 / 1 against its 1 / 1 / 1.

WHAT THE EXTRA CROSSINGS COST ON .NET: nothing measurable.
P1.2 encode, non-counting core, same process class:
  AK_CHUNK=0    156.83 ns/element   2 forward crossings
  AK_CHUNK=150  157.95 ns/element   8 forward crossings
0.7 percent apart, inside the run-to-run spread. Six extra crossings over a 218 KB
payload is about 60 ns in total at .NET's 7.5-12 ns, which is 0.06 ns/element.

The reading that matters: "make the crossings fewer, not cheaper" is a rule about
crossings that scale with FIELD COUNT -- the drafted ABI's 15,137 to decode a
thousand rows -- and not about the difference between 2 and 8. Once the batching
predicate admits a message, chunk size is free on .NET and the host should pick it
for memory rather than for crossings: the whole-run form needs a group array
proportional to the element count (1,000 x 200 bytes = 200 KB on P1.2), the
chunked form needs 150 x 200 = 30 KB whatever the payload.
